  "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $6+\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $6+\\sqrt{6},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6-\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6-\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{6+\\sqrt{6}}{6+\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}(6+\\sqrt{6})}{{6}^{2}-(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{18}}{36-6} & = \\\\ \\frac{6\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{9\\cdot 2}}{30} & = \\\\ \\frac{6\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{2}}{30} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
